.SH "NAME"
pcre2_match \- match a compiled pattern to a subject string (Perl compatible)
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
\fB\&#include\fP \fB<pcre2.h>\fP

\fBint\fP pcre2_match(\fBconst\fP \fBpcre2_code\fP *\fIcode\fP, \fBPCRE2_SPTR\fP \fIsubject\fP,
                \fBPCRE2_SIZE\fP \fIlength\fP, \fBPCRE2_SIZE\fP \fIstart_offset\fP,
                \fBuint32_t\fP \fIoptions\fP, \fBpcre2_match_data\fP *\fImatch_data\fP,
                \fBpcre2_match_context\fP *\fImcontext\fP);
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
This function matches a compiled regular expression against a given subject
string, using a matching algorithm that is similar to Perl\(cqs. It returns
offsets to what it has matched and to captured substrings via the
\fImatch_data\fP block, which can be processed by functions with names that
start with \f(CRpcre2_get_ovector_...()\fP or \f(CRpcre2_substring_...()\fP. The
return from pcre2_match() is one more than the highest numbered capturing
pair that has been set (for example, 1 if there are no captures), zero if the
vector of offsets is too small, or a negative error code for no match and other
errors. The function arguments are:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 13
\fIcode\fP
.ad l
Points to the compiled pattern
.TP 13
\fIsubject\fP
.ad l
Points to the subject string
.TP 13
\fIlength\fP
.ad l
Length of the subject string
.TP 13
\fIstart_offset\fP
.ad l
Offset in the subject at which to start matching
.TP 13
\fIoptions\fP
.ad l
Option bits
.TP 13
\fImatch_data\fP
.ad l
Points to a match data block, for results
.TP 13
\fImcontext\fP
.ad l
Points to a match context, or is \fBNULL\fP
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
A match context is needed only if you want to:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Set up a callout function
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Set a matching offset limit
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Change the heap memory limit
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Change the backtracking match limit
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Change the backtracking depth limit
.RE
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Set custom memory management specifically for the match
.RE
.sp
The \fIlength\fP and \fIstart_offset\fP values are code units, not characters.
The length may be given as \fBPCRE2_ZERO_TERMINATED\fP for a subject that is
terminated by a binary zero code unit. The options are:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 23
\fBPCRE2_ANCHORED\fP
.ad l
Match only at the first position
.TP 23
\fBPCRE2_COPY_MATCHED_SUBJECT\fP
.ad l
On success, make a private subject copy
.TP 23
\fBPCRE2_DISABLE_RECURSELOOP_CHECK\fP
.ad l
Only useful in rare cases; use with care
.TP 23
\fBPCRE2_ENDANCHORED\fP
.ad l
Pattern can match only at end of subject
.TP 23
\fBPCRE2_NOTBOL\fP
.ad l
Subject string is not the beginning of a line
.TP 23
\fBPCRE2_NOTEOL\fP
.ad l
Subject string is not the end of a line
.TP 23
\fBPCRE2_NOTEMPTY\fP
.ad l
An empty string is not a valid match
.TP 23
\fBPCRE2_NOTEMPTY_ATSTART\fP
.ad l
An empty string at the start of the subject is not a valid match
.TP 23
\fBPCRE2_NO_JIT\fP
.ad l
Do not use JIT matching
.TP 23
\fBPCRE2_NO_UTF_CHECK\fP
.ad l
Do not check the subject for UTF validity (only relevant if
\fBPCRE2_UTF\fP was set at compile time)
.TP 23
\fBPCRE2_PARTIAL_HARD\fP
.ad l
Return \fBPCRE2_ERROR_PARTIAL\fP for a partial match even if there is a
full match
.TP 23
\fBPCRE2_PARTIAL_SOFT\fP
.ad l
Return \fBPCRE2_ERROR_PARTIAL\fP for a partial match if no full matches
are found
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
For details of partial matching, see the
\f(CRpcre2partial\fP
page. There is a complete description of the PCRE2 native API in the
\f(CRpcre2api\fP
page and a description of the POSIX API in the
\f(CRpcre2posix\fP
page.